Imports:
  - Types:
      - Logger
    From: BrowserKit/Sources/Common/Logger

Usages:
  legacy_modern_migration: |
    The app is mid-migration from an older Action/ActionType pair to a newer, simpler
    ModernAction protocol. Every Reducer and Middleware therefore carries both a
    legacyReducer/legacyMiddleware half (driven by Action) and a modernReducer/modernMiddleware
    half (driven by ModernAction), and Store runs whichever half matches the kind of action that
    was dispatched. New feature code should prefer ModernAction; existing Action conformances
    are not required to migrate immediately.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the app's unidirectional-data-flow framework: a single, app-wide `Store` holds
  one `StateType` value; `Action`/`ModernAction` values are the only way to request a change;
  reducer functions are the only place state is actually computed; middleware functions observe
  the new state and action to run side effects (logging, persistence, analytics, etc.);
  `StoreSubscriber`s are notified only when the state they selected actually changed.

  Use `legacy_modern_migration` before adding a new action/reducer/middleware pair.

---

"StateType()":
  location: State.swift
  annotations: |
    Contract every piece of state held in a `Store` must satisfy.
  methods:
    "defaultState(from state: Self) -> defaultState:Self": |
      Return a copy of `state` with every transient field reset to its default, preserving
      everything else. The single source of truth for what counts as "transient" for this type.
    "resetTransientState() -> state:Self": |
      Convenience equivalent to calling defaultState with self; available for free once
      defaultState is implemented.

"Action()":
  location: Action.swift
  annotations: |
    Legacy action contract. Every dispatched action carries the window it originated in and a
    type tag used by reducers/middlewares to switch on.

    Use `legacy_modern_migration` to decide whether new code should conform to this instead of
    ModernAction.
  properties:
    "windowUUID -> WindowUUID": |
      The window (for multi-window iPad) this action originated in or targets.
    "actionType -> ActionType": |
      Type tag identifying which concrete action case this is, for reducer/middleware dispatch.

"ModernAction()":
  location: Action.swift
  annotations: |
    Replacement for Action/ActionType, without the window/type-tag ceremony; dispatched together
    with an explicit windowUUID argument at the call site instead.

    Use `legacy_modern_migration` for context on why both protocols currently coexist.
  properties:
    "description -> String": |
      Human-readable debug description; auto-derived by default from the action's enum case name
      and associated values via reflection.

"DispatchStore()":
  location: DispatchStore.swift
  annotations: |
    Minimal contract for "something actions can be dispatched to". Runs on the main actor.
  methods:
    "dispatch(action: Action)": |
      Legacy dispatch path.

      Use `legacy_modern_migration` for when to prefer this vs. the `ModernAction` overload.
    "dispatch(action: ModernAction, forWindowUUID windowUUID: WindowUUID)": |
      Modern dispatch path.

      `windowUUID`: the window this action originates in or targets

"DispatchStore::DefaultDispatchStore()":
  location: DispatchStore.swift
  annotations: |
    Adds subscription management on top of `DispatchStore`, parameterized by the concrete
    `StateType` the store holds.
  properties:
    "state -> State": |
      Current state snapshot.
  methods:
    "subscribe(subscriber: S)": |
      Subscribe to the full state; `subscriber` is notified on every change.

      `subscriber`: a `StoreSubscriber` whose subscriber state type matches the store's State
    "subscribe(subscriber: S, transform: SubstateTransform)": |
      Subscribe to a derived slice of the state, built via `Subscription`'s select operation;
      `subscriber` is notified only when that slice changes.

      `subscriber`: a `StoreSubscriber` whose subscriber state type matches the derived sub-state
      `transform`: a function building the sub-state `Subscription` from the root `Subscription`
    "unsubscribe(subscriber: S)": |
      Remove a previously added typed subscriber.
    "unsubscribe(subscriber: StoreSubscriber)": |
      Remove a previously added subscriber by its erased identity.

"DefaultDispatchStore::Store(state: State, reducer: Reducer, middlewares: [Middleware], logger: Logger)":
  location: Store.swift
  annotations: |
    The concrete, app-wide state container. Holds one `state` value, one `reducer`, and zero or
    more `middlewares`. Dispatched actions are queued and drained one at a time so a single
    action fully finishes its reducer-then-middlewares pass before the next one starts.

    `state`: initial state value
    `reducer`: the reducer used to compute new state (see the `Reducer` routine in this cell)
    `middlewares`: side-effect handlers run after each state update (see the `Middleware`
    routine in this cell)
    `logger`: diagnostic logger; defaults to the app-wide `Logger` singleton

    Algorithm:
    1. On dispatch, wrap the action and append it to an internal FIFO queue together with its
       windowUUID
    2. If a drain is not already running, start draining: pop the next queued action, run it
       fully through step 3, repeat until the queue is empty
    3. For the popped action: compute new state via the matching (legacy/modern) half of
       `reducer`, then run every middleware's matching half against the new state, then publish
       the new state to `state`
    4. Publishing `state` notifies every live subscription whose selected value actually changed;
       dead (deallocated) subscribers are pruned first
  methods:
    "dispatch(action: Action)": |
      Queue a legacy action for processing.
    "dispatch(action: ModernAction, forWindowUUID windowUUID: WindowUUID)": |
      Queue a modern action for processing.

"StoreSubscriber()":
  location: StoreSubscriber.swift
  annotations: |
    A listener notified with a typed state slice whenever that slice changes.
  methods:
    "newState(state: SubscriberStateType)": |
      Called with the new value of the subscriber's selected state slice, only when it differs
      from the previous value.

"Subscription()":
  location: Subscription.swift
  annotations: |
    Represents one path from the root state down to a value a subscriber cares about; created
    internally when subscribing to a `Store` and refined via its select operation.
  properties:
    "observer -> ((State?, State) -> Void)?": |
      Callback invoked with the old and new value whenever they differ.
  methods:
    "select(selector: StateSelector) -> subscription:Subscription": |
      Derive a subscription to a smaller slice of this subscription's state, so the resulting
      subscriber is only notified when that slice changes.

      `selector`: a function projecting the parent state down to the slice of interest

"Reducer(state: State, action: ModernAction, windowUUID: WindowUUID) -> newState:State":
  location: Reducer.swift
  annotations: |
    The shape every reducer must have: a pure function from the current state and a dispatched
    action to the next state. Never perform side effects here; that is the `Middleware` routine's
    job.

    Use `legacy_modern_migration`: a real reducer value is a pair of functions with this same
    shape — one for `Action` (without windowUUID as a parameter, taking it from the action
    itself) and one for `ModernAction` (taking windowUUID explicitly, as shown here).

"Middleware(state: State, action: ModernAction, windowUUID: WindowUUID)":
  location: Middleware.swift
  annotations: |
    The shape every middleware must have: observe the already-updated state and the action that
    produced it, and perform a side effect (logging, persistence, analytics, triggering a
    follow-up dispatch, etc.). Must not mutate `state` directly; if a side effect should change
    state, it dispatches a new action instead.

    Use `legacy_modern_migration`: a real middleware value is likewise a pair of functions, one
    per action kind, exactly as the `Reducer` routine is.

